> **Note (maintainers):** this is a *precomputed* vignette. The results and figures below are baked in from a local run of the full study; the shipped `.Rmd` is not re-executed at build time. Edit `spiDE-simulation.Rmd.orig` and re-run `vignettes/precompile.R` to regenerate. Timings and sweep values are snapshots of the machine that built them.

# Overview

This is a **research/benchmarking vignette**, not user-facing documentation of the package API. It answers a concrete question that comes up when exploring `spiDE`'s mixed-effects correction (`random = "intercept"` / `"slope"`, see the companion vignette *The spiDE model: neighbourhood-dependent DE and mixed-effects inference*): if I simulate more samples, or more cells per sample, why doesn't power obviously go up?

The short answer, demonstrated quantitatively below: statistical power under the mixed-effects correction is driven almost entirely by the number of **samples** (patients), not the number of **cells**. That is not a bug — it is the price of correcting cell-level pseudo-replication. Cells within a sample share the same response label, so they cannot contribute independent evidence about a between-sample contrast; only more samples can. The bundled toy dataset (`.toySPE()` / `data(toySpiDE)`) obscures this because (a) it reseeds the *entire* dataset when you change `n_samples`/`n_per`, so effect size is confounded with sample size, and (b) it only plants one true gene among \~20, so the gene-level FDR gate swallows the signal. Here we build a **fair** simulation harness that isolates the two questions: does the null stay calibrated, and does power scale correctly with each knob.

All code needed to reproduce this study is self-contained in this file (no internal `spiDE:::` helpers are used) so it can be lifted out and run independently of the package if desired.

```{r load, message = FALSE}
library(spiDE)
library(SpatialExperiment)
library(BiocParallel)
library(ggplot2)

# keep the default vignette build light; bump these for a real study (see the
# final section for suggested "full" settings and expected runtime)
RUN_FULL <- TRUE
B <- if (RUN_FULL) 40 else 6              # replicate seeds per design point
NPAR <- if (RUN_FULL) 10 else 1           # parallel workers (cores available)
BPPARAM <- if (NPAR > 1) MulticoreParam(NPAR, RNGseed = 1) else SerialParam()
set.seed(1)  # fitSpiDE is stochastic (fitNB subsamples cells for dispersion)
```

`MulticoreParam` parallelises by forking. SpaNorm's GPU path (used by `fitSpiDE()` when `backend = "auto"`, the default) initialises `torch`'s Metal/MPS runtime, and that runtime is **not fork-safe** — a forked worker crashes with `+[MPSGraphObject initialize] ... fork() ... Crashing instead`. All fits below therefore pass `backend = "cpu"`, which keeps SpaNorm off the GPU so the forked workers are safe. (The models here are small; the CPU path is the right choice anyway. On a machine with a usable GPU, keep the GPU by parallelising with the non-forking `SnowParam(NPAR)` instead.)

# Data generators

Both generators plant a **patient-level random intercept** (`sd_patient`) on top of the cell-type baseline, so — unlike `.toySPE()` — every simulated dataset has genuine between-sample clustering, the actual source of pseudo-replication. `sim_null()` has no response effect at all (used for Type-I error / calibration); `sim_alt()` additionally plants a known, gene-indexed A-index / B-niche response effect in a specified fraction of genes (used for power and FDR).

```{r generators}
.simulate_spe <- function(S, n_per, n_genes, field, sd_patient, seed,
                          nonnull = integer(0), beta = 0, sd_slope = 0) {
  set.seed(seed)
  cts <- c("A", "B", "C")
  gn <- sprintf("G%d", seq_len(n_genes))
  sids <- sprintf("S%d", seq_len(S))
  cond <- rep(c("Responder", "Non-responder"), length.out = S)
  names(cond) <- sids

  cells <- lapply(sids, function(sid) {
    x <- runif(n_per, 0, field)
    y <- runif(n_per, 0, field)
    ct <- ifelse(x > 0.6 * field & runif(n_per) < 0.7, "B",
                sample(c("A", "C"), n_per, replace = TRUE))
    data.frame(sample_id = sid, condition = cond[[sid]],
              x = x, y = y, cell_type = ct, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
  })
  cd <- do.call(rbind, cells)
  n <- nrow(cd)
  cd$cell_id <- sprintf("c%d", seq_len(n))

  # per-cell-type baseline log-mean
  base <- matrix(rnorm(n_genes * length(cts), 1.5, 0.6), n_genes,
                 dimnames = list(gn, cts))
  lmu <- base[, cd$cell_type, drop = FALSE]

  # per-(gene, sample) random intercept: real clustering, no response effect
  u <- matrix(rnorm(n_genes * S, 0, sd_patient), n_genes,
             dimnames = list(gn, sids))
  lmu <- lmu + u[, cd$sample_id, drop = FALSE]

  # planted A-index / B-niche response effect in the `nonnull` genes; x/field
  # is a proxy for local B-niche density (B cells sit at high x)
  if (length(nonnull) > 0 && beta != 0) {
    is_A <- cd$cell_type == "A"
    is_resp <- cd$condition == "Responder"
    slope_shift <- if (sd_slope > 0) {
      # per-sample random slope noise, shared across the nonnull genes'
      # per-sample deviation but independent across samples
      v <- setNames(rnorm(S, 0, sd_slope), sids)
      v[cd$sample_id]
    } else 0
    eff <- (beta + slope_shift) * is_A * is_resp * (cd$x / field)
    for (g in nonnull) lmu[g, ] <- lmu[g, ] + eff
  }

  mu <- exp(lmu)
  counts <- matrix(rnbinom(length(mu), mu = as.vector(mu), size = 5), n_genes,
                   dimnames = list(gn, cd$cell_id))
  cd$Area <- exp(rnorm(n, sd = 0.2))
  cd$nCount <- colSums(counts)

  SpatialExperiment(assays = list(counts = counts),
                    colData = S4Vectors::DataFrame(cd),
                    spatialCoords = as.matrix(cd[, c("x", "y")]))
}

sim_null <- function(S = 10, n_per = 90, n_genes = 30, field = 500,
                     sd_patient = 0.7, seed = 1) {
  .simulate_spe(S, n_per, n_genes, field, sd_patient, seed)
}

sim_alt <- function(S = 10, n_per = 90, n_genes = 150, field = 500,
                    prop_nonnull = 0.15, beta = 2, sd_patient = 0.7,
                    sd_slope = 0, seed = 1) {
  n_nonnull <- max(1, round(prop_nonnull * n_genes))
  nonnull <- seq_len(n_nonnull) # G1..Gk are the true positives, by construction
  spe <- .simulate_spe(S, n_per, n_genes, field, sd_patient, seed,
                       nonnull = nonnull, beta = beta, sd_slope = sd_slope)
  list(spe = spe, truth = seq_len(n_genes) %in% nonnull, nonnull_genes = nonnull)
}
```

# What a simulated dataset looks like

Before running any fits, it helps to *see* the two structural features the study relies on: the **spatial niche gradient** (B cells cluster at high `x`, so `x/field` is a proxy for local B-density) and the **planted signal** (gene `G1` is up-regulated in `A` (index) cells, in *Responder* samples, in proportion to that B-niche gradient). We generate one deliberately large dataset purely for visualisation.

```{r viz-data, message = FALSE}
demo <- sim_alt(S = 6, n_per = 500, beta = 2, seed = 1)
vd <- as.data.frame(colData(demo$spe))
xy <- spatialCoords(demo$spe)
vd$x <- xy[, 1]
vd$y <- xy[, 2]
# G1 is the first planted true-positive gene (nonnull_genes == G1..Gk)
vd$G1 <- log1p(as.numeric(SummarizedExperiment::assay(demo$spe, "counts")["G1", ]))
vd$is_A <- ifelse(vd$cell_type == "A", "A (index)", "other cell types")
```

## Cell-type layout in an example sample

Each cell is placed uniformly in a square field, but `B` cells are enriched at high `x` (`x > 0.6 * field`). This is the spatial gradient the niche density is built from — the effective B-niche estimated by `buildNiches()` increases from left to right.

```{r viz-celltypes, fig.width = 5.5, fig.height = 4.5, fig.cap = "Spatial layout of the three cell types in one example sample (S1). B cells concentrate at high x; A and C cells are spread uniformly. The left-to-right B gradient is what the niche-density covariate captures."}
ggplot(subset(vd, sample_id == "S1"), aes(x, y, colour = cell_type)) +
  geom_point(size = 1) +
  coord_equal() +
  labs(title = "Cell-type layout (sample S1)", colour = "cell type") +
  theme_bw()
```

## The planted signal in an example gene

The effect is a three-way interaction: expression of `G1` is lifted only where a cell is **type A**, **in a Responder sample**, and **at high B-niche density (high x)**. Splitting the spatial map by cell type (rows) and by an example Responder / Non-responder sample (columns) isolates it — only the top-left panel (A cells, Responder) shows a left-to-right expression gradient; every other panel is flat.

```{r viz-signal, fig.width = 7.5, fig.height = 6, fig.cap = "Spatial distribution of G1 expression in an example Responder (S1) and Non-responder (S2) sample, split by whether the cell is the index type A. The high-x expression gradient appears only for A cells in the responder -- exactly the celltype:condition:niche effect spiDE tests for."}
ggplot(subset(vd, sample_id %in% c("S1", "S2")), aes(x, y, colour = G1)) +
  geom_point(size = 1.3) +
  scale_colour_viridis_c(option = "magma") +
  facet_grid(is_A ~ paste0(sample_id, " (", condition, ")")) +
  coord_equal() +
  labs(title = "Planted A-index / B-niche response signal (gene G1)",
       colour = "log1p(G1)") +
  theme_bw()
```

# Shared driver

For a given simulated dataset we fit all three `random` modes at a single bandwidth and pull out two levels of evidence:

-   **raw Wald p-values** on the `ResponseNiche` (A-index x B-niche) coefficient, gate-free — the cleanest signal for calibration/power curves;
-   **end-to-end discoveries** from `results()`, gated on the reported FDR q-values — what a user actually sees.

Two efficiency ideas make the sweeps below cheap enough to run at scale, and are worth stating explicitly because they shape all of the code:

1.  **Fit each dataset once, derive every metric from it.** `buildNiches()` is method-independent, so it is computed a single time per dataset and shared across the three `random` modes. And `testSpiDE()` called at `fdr = 1` returns the *full* `(gene, index, niche)` table with its `fdr.gene`/`fdr.index`/`fdr.niche` q-values; because the niche-level Benjamini–Hochberg step is computed *within* each `gene × index` group, gating those three columns at any target `t` reproduces `testSpiDE(fdr = t)` exactly. So one fit yields the raw-p **power**, the realized **FDP**, the **recall (TPR)** at every threshold, and any other end-to-end metric — no model is ever re-fit to change a threshold. (Parts B and C in earlier versions of this vignette fit two nearly identical sets of `sim_alt()` models; they are now a single set of fits.)
2.  **Parallelise over datasets, not over the grid.** Every metric is a mean over `B` independent simulated datasets at each design point. We therefore flatten the whole study into one long list of *(design point, replicate)* jobs and hand that to `bplapply`, so all `r NPAR` workers stay busy and the wildly uneven per-job cost (a 30-sample / 640-cell fit dwarfs a 4-sample one) load-balances. The old code parallelised over the ~5 grid points only, leaving most cores idle.

```{r driver}
sigma_fixed <- 50
methods <- c(fixed = "none", intercept = "intercept", slope = "slope")

# raw two-sided Wald p-value for the A-index x B-niche ResponseNiche coefficient
.raw_AB_p <- function(fit1) {
  cn <- colnames(fit1@t_stat)
  col <- which(grepl("CellTypeA", cn) & grepl("B$", cn) & grepl("Responder", cn))
  stopifnot(length(col) == 1)
  df <- if (is.null(fit1@df)) Inf else fit1@df
  2 * stats::pt(-abs(fit1@t_stat[, col]), df)
}

# Fit ONE dataset once per `random` mode and return everything downstream needs.
# buildNiches is shared across modes; testSpiDE is called at fdr = 1 so the full
# q-value table can be re-gated at any target (see note above). backend = "cpu"
# keeps SpaNorm off the fork-unsafe GPU inside the parallel workers.
.fit_all <- function(spe, mods = methods) {
  spe <- buildNiches(spe, sigma = sigma_fixed)
  lapply(mods, function(m) {
    fit <- fitSpiDE(spe, "condition", sigma = sigma_fixed, random = m,
                    backend = "cpu", verbose = FALSE, re.prop = 0.1)
    res <- testSpiDE(fit, spe = spe, fdr = 1)
    tab <- results(res)
    list(raw_p = .raw_AB_p(fits(res)[[1]]),
         ab = tab[tab$ct_index == "A" & tab$ct_niche == "B", , drop = FALSE])
  })
}

# end-to-end A/B discoveries at FDR target t, re-gated from a single fdr = 1 table
.discoveries <- function(ab, t) {
  unique(ab$gene[ab$fdr.gene <= t & ab$fdr.index <= t & ab$fdr.niche <= t])
}

# Run a data.frame of jobs (one row = one simulated dataset) across all workers.
run_jobs <- function(grid, fun) {
  jl <- split(grid, seq_len(nrow(grid)))
  do.call(rbind, bplapply(jl, function(j) do.call(fun, as.list(j)),
                          BPPARAM = BPPARAM))
}
```

# Part A — calibration under the null

No response effect is planted (`sim_null()`); every response test should be null, so p-values should be $\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)$ and the rejection rate at $\alpha = 0.05$ should be $\approx 0.05$ for a correctly calibrated method.

```{r partA-run}
S_grid_null <- if (RUN_FULL) c(4, 6, 10, 16, 24) else c(6, 16)

jobA <- function(S, b) {
  fa <- .fit_all(sim_null(S = S, seed = 1000 * S + b))
  data.frame(S = S, method = names(fa),
             reject_rate = vapply(fa, function(x) mean(x$raw_p < 0.05), numeric(1)),
             row.names = NULL)
}
partA_raw <- run_jobs(expand.grid(S = S_grid_null, b = seq_len(B)), jobA)
partA <- aggregate(reject_rate ~ S + method, partA_raw, mean)
partA
```

```{r partA-plot, fig.cap = "Type-I error (rejection rate at alpha=0.05) under the null, by number of samples S. Dashed line = nominal 0.05. The fixed-effects fit is inflated regardless of S; the mixed-effects fits are close to nominal and improve as S grows."}
ggplot(partA, aes(S, reject_rate, colour = method)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) +
  scale_y_log10() +
  labs(y = "P(raw p < 0.05 | null)", title = "Null calibration vs number of samples") +
  theme_bw()
```

A QQ-plot makes the same point at the level of the full p-value distribution rather than a single threshold. Two subtleties matter for reading it correctly, and a *single* simulated dataset gets both wrong:

-   The variance components (`tau2`) are **shared across genes and estimated from only `S` samples**, so all `n_genes` response-niche p-values in one dataset ride on the *same* `tau2` estimate. One unlucky variance-component draw tilts the entire curve — the departure is coherent, not gene-by-gene noise. This is most pronounced for `random = "slope"`, whose extra per-sample slope variance is the least stable component. So we **pool over all `B` datasets**.
-   `fitSpiDE()` is **not deterministic by default**: `fitNB` subsamples cells for the dispersion estimate using the RNG, so the same data + model can give visibly different p-values run to run. We therefore `set.seed()` before every fit (identically across the three methods, so they see the same draw).

```{r partA-qq, fig.cap = "QQ-plot of null response-niche p-values at S = 10, pooled over all B datasets with the fit RNG fixed. The fixed-effects fit is badly anti-conservative; the mixed-effects fits are far closer to the uniform (dashed) reference, though both remain mildly anti-conservative at this small S (the residual tail shrinks as S grows, cf. Part A)."}
qq_raw <- run_jobs(
  expand.grid(b = seq_len(B), method = names(methods), stringsAsFactors = FALSE),
  function(b, method) {
    spe <- buildNiches(sim_null(S = 10, seed = 7000 + b), sigma = sigma_fixed)
    set.seed(b) # identical draw across methods, so they see the same subsample
    fit <- fitSpiDE(spe, "condition", sigma = sigma_fixed,
                    random = methods[[method]], backend = "cpu",
                    verbose = FALSE, re.prop = 0.1)
    res <- testSpiDE(fit, spe = spe, fdr = 1)
    data.frame(method = method, p = .raw_AB_p(fits(res)[[1]]), row.names = NULL)
  })
qq_df <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(methods), function(m) {
  p <- sort(qq_raw$p[qq_raw$method == m])
  data.frame(method = m, expected = -log10(ppoints(length(p))),
             observed = -log10(p))
}))
ggplot(qq_df, aes(expected, observed, colour = method)) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_point(size = 1, alpha = 0.5) +
  labs(x = "expected -log10(p)", y = "observed -log10(p)") +
  theme_bw()
```

Two practical takeaways from this null study:

-   **Reproducibility, not just averaging.** Because `fitNB` subsamples cells for the dispersion estimate from the RNG, the same data and model can give materially different response-niche p-values (and `tau2` estimates) from run to run. Set a seed before fitting — as done above and in the setup chunk — if you need reproducible variance components. (Raising `fitNB`'s `maxn.psi` to cover all cells removes the subsampling entirely and makes the fit deterministic, at some cost.)
-   **`random = "slope"` is the less stable model at small `S`.** Its extra per-sample slope variance is collinear with the tested fixed effect and is estimated from only `S` samples, so it is heavy-tailed and, per dataset, tends to be a little more anti-conservative than `"intercept"` (visible as the slope curve sitting slightly above intercept). Both converge to nominal as `S` grows (Part A); flooring or clamping `tau2` does *not* fix the small-`S` gap. Prefer `"intercept"` when you have few samples and no reason to expect the niche-slope itself to vary between patients.

# Parts B & C — power, FDR and recall from one set of fits

Now we plant a real effect (`sim_alt()`, `beta = 2`, 15% of genes true) and answer the power *and* the FDR-control questions **from the same fits**. We define a set of design points — an `S`-sweep at fixed cells/sample, a cells/sample-sweep at fixed `S`, and a reference point used for the FDR-threshold curves — de-duplicated so a shared point is fit only once. Each *(design point, replicate)* dataset is fit once per method (via `.fit_all()`), and from that single fit we read off the raw-p power and the whole realized-FDP / recall curve over `fdr_grid` (via `.discoveries()`).

```{r partBC-run}
S_grid   <- if (RUN_FULL) c(4, 8, 12, 20, 30) else c(6, 12, 24)
npc_grid <- if (RUN_FULL) c(40, 80, 160, 320, 640) else c(80, 200, 400)
fdr_grid <- if (RUN_FULL) c(0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3) else c(0.05, 0.2)
S_ref    <- if (RUN_FULL) 16 else 12   # reference point for the FDR curves
S_fixed  <- 10                         # held fixed while sweeping cells/sample
npc_fixed <- 90                        # held fixed while sweeping samples

# design points, de-duplicated so a point shared by two sweeps is fit only once
design <- unique(rbind(
  data.frame(S = S_grid,  n_per = npc_fixed),
  data.frame(S = S_fixed, n_per = npc_grid),
  data.frame(S = S_ref,   n_per = npc_fixed)
))
gridBC <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(design)), function(i)
  data.frame(S = design$S[i], n_per = design$n_per[i], b = seq_len(B))))

jobBC <- function(S, n_per, b) {
  sim <- sim_alt(S = S, n_per = n_per, n_genes = 150, prop_nonnull = 0.15,
                 beta = 2, seed = S * 1e6 + n_per * 1e3 + b)
  truth <- sprintf("G%d", sim$nonnull_genes)
  fa <- .fit_all(sim$spe)
  do.call(rbind, lapply(names(fa), function(m) {
    x <- fa[[m]]
    per_fdr <- do.call(rbind, lapply(fdr_grid, function(t) {
      hit <- .discoveries(x$ab, t)
      data.frame(fdr = t,
                 fdp = if (length(hit)) mean(!(hit %in% truth)) else 0,
                 tpr = mean(truth %in% hit))
    }))
    data.frame(S = S, n_per = n_per, b = b, method = m,
               power_raw = mean(x$raw_p[sim$nonnull_genes] < 0.05),
               per_fdr, row.names = NULL)
  }))
}

simBC <- run_jobs(gridBC, jobBC)

# power_raw is constant across the fdr rows of a fit; one row per fit for it
fitrows <- subset(simBC, fdr == fdr_grid[[1]])
powerS <- aggregate(power_raw ~ S + method,
                    subset(fitrows, n_per == npc_fixed & S %in% S_grid), mean)
powerN <- aggregate(power_raw ~ n_per + method,
                    subset(fitrows, S == S_fixed & n_per %in% npc_grid), mean)
```

## Power: samples buy it, cells do not

Power is the fraction of the true genes recovered as raw-p `< 0.05`. The two panels are the whole answer to the motivating question: power climbs with `S` but is flat (only weakly increasing, via the dispersion estimate) with `n_per`.

```{r partBC-plot-S, fig.cap = "Power to detect the planted A-index/B-niche effect vs number of samples, at fixed cells/sample. Power rises for the mixed-effects methods as S grows."}
ggplot(powerS, aes(S, power_raw, colour = method)) +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "number of samples (S)", y = "power (P(raw p < 0.05 | true effect))") +
  theme_bw()
```

```{r partBC-plot-npc, fig.cap = "Power vs cells per sample, at fixed S. Under the mixed-effects correction power is essentially flat in cell count -- extra within-sample cells cannot substitute for extra samples, because the response label is constant within a sample."}
ggplot(powerN, aes(n_per, power_raw, colour = method)) +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "cells per sample", y = "power (P(raw p < 0.05 | true effect))") +
  theme_bw()
```

The fixed-effects curve looks "more powerful" in both panels, but Part A already showed its apparent power is inflated by the same anti-conservative bias that broke its null calibration — comparing raw power across methods is only meaningful once Type-I error is matched, which the realized-FDR curves below do properly.

## FDR and recall vs the nominal threshold

At the reference design point we plot, from the very same fits, whether the *realized* false discovery proportion (FDP) tracks the nominal threshold and how many true positives are recovered (TPR) at each.

```{r partBC-fdr-agg}
ref <- subset(simBC, S == S_ref & n_per == npc_fixed)
fdp_curve <- aggregate(fdp ~ fdr + method, ref, mean)
tpr_curve <- aggregate(tpr ~ fdr + method, ref, mean)
```

```{r partBC-plot-fdr, fig.cap = "Realized false discovery proportion (top) and true-positive rate (bottom) vs the nominal FDR threshold, at the reference design point. The dashed diagonal in the top panel is the y=x reference: points below it mean FDR is controlled; above it means it is exceeded."}
p1 <- ggplot(fdp_curve, aes(fdr, fdp, colour = method)) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) +
  labs(x = "nominal FDR", y = "realized FDP") + theme_bw()
p2 <- ggplot(tpr_curve, aes(fdr, tpr, colour = method)) +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "nominal FDR", y = "TPR (recall)") + theme_bw()
p1
p2
```

## A metric that comes for free: end-to-end recall vs samples

Because every fit already carries its full q-value table, we can read off metrics the original two-part split could not without yet more fits — for instance the **end-to-end** discovery recall (TPR at a fixed `fdr = 0.05`, i.e. what a user actually gets after gene→index→niche gating) as a function of `S`, alongside the gate-free raw-p power. The gap between them is the price of the hierarchical FDR gate.

```{r partBC-plot-e2e, fig.cap = "Raw-p power (dashed) vs end-to-end discovery recall at FDR = 0.05 (solid) as samples grow, for the mixed-effects fits. Both come from the same set of fits; the gap is the cost of the gene-level FDR gate."}
e2e <- aggregate(tpr ~ S + method,
                 subset(simBC, n_per == npc_fixed & S %in% S_grid & fdr == 0.05), mean)
ggplot(subset(powerS, method != "fixed"), aes(S, power_raw, colour = method)) +
  geom_line(linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_line(data = subset(e2e, method != "fixed"), aes(S, tpr)) +
  geom_point(data = subset(e2e, method != "fixed"), aes(S, tpr), size = 2) +
  ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "number of samples (S)",
       y = "recall — raw-p (dashed) vs end-to-end at FDR 0.05 (solid)") +
  theme_bw()
```

# Part D — intercept vs slope: the few-responders trade-off

The model vignette notes a trade-off: `random = "slope"` gives the fullest protection against pseudo-replication, but with few samples the random slope can absorb a genuine effect, because both describe between-sample variation in the same niche-dependent slope. We reproduce that here by shrinking `S` at fixed effect size and comparing `"intercept"` vs `"slope"`.

```{r partD-run}
S_small_grid <- if (RUN_FULL) c(4, 6, 8, 12) else c(4, 8)

jobD <- function(S, b) {
  sim <- sim_alt(S = S, n_genes = 60, prop_nonnull = 0.15, beta = 2.5,
                 sd_slope = 0.4, seed = 4000 * S + b)
  fa <- .fit_all(sim$spe, mods = c(intercept = "intercept", slope = "slope"))
  data.frame(S = S, method = names(fa),
             power = vapply(fa, function(x)
               mean(x$raw_p[sim$nonnull_genes] < 0.05), numeric(1)),
             row.names = NULL)
}
partD_raw <- run_jobs(expand.grid(S = S_small_grid, b = seq_len(B)), jobD)
partD <- aggregate(power ~ S + method, partD_raw, mean)
```

```{r partD-plot, fig.cap = "Power of random='intercept' vs random='slope' as the number of samples shrinks, with genuine per-sample slope heterogeneity present. With few samples, the random slope can absorb part of the true effect, making 'intercept' the more powerful (if less fully protective) default; the gap narrows as S grows."}
ggplot(partD, aes(S, power, colour = method)) +
  geom_line() + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "number of samples (S)", y = "power") + theme_bw()
```

# Discussion

Putting the pieces together answers the original question -- "I increased samples and cells and power didn't move":

1.  **Cells cannot buy power under the mixed-effects correction, by design** (Parts B&C, power-vs-cells panel). The response label is constant within a sample, so extra cells are pseudo-replicates; the Wald test for the response-niche effect is evaluated in the between-sample stratum (`df = S - 2`, and standard errors that floor at the between-sample variance scale), matching the between-sample degrees of freedom documented in the model vignette.
2.  **Samples do buy power** (Parts B&C, power-vs-samples panel), and this study isolates that cleanly because `beta`/`sd_patient`/seeds are held structurally fixed across the sweep -- something the bundled toy generator does not do, since it draws its entire dataset (coordinates, cell types, baselines, noise) from one `set.seed()` call that depends on `n_samples`/`n_per` themselves.
3.  **The fixed-effects fit's apparent extra "power" is not real power** -- Part A shows it is badly anti-conservative under the null regardless of sample size, so any power comparison against it is unfair until Type-I error is matched; the realized-FDR curves in Parts B&C make the honest comparison and show the mixed-effects fits controlling FDR near the nominal level while the fixed-effects fit overshoots.
4.  **Gene-level gating can hide a real, well-powered niche-level hit.** In the original toy example the gene-level FDR was pinned near 1 (Brown-combining one strong interaction with several null ones, then BH over only \~20 genes) even when the niche-level test (`fdr.niche`) was already strongly significant. This study reports power/FDR at the niche level throughout to avoid that dilution; the FDR curves confirm the same gating still yields controlled FDR when there are enough true positives to estimate it, and the end-to-end recall panel shows what the gate costs.
5.  **With very few samples, `random = "slope"` can cost power relative to `"intercept"`** (Part D), because a real per-sample slope effect is statistically hard to distinguish from slope noise when there are only a handful of patients to average over.

## Scaling up this study

This vignette is shipped precomputed from a `RUN_FULL <- TRUE` run (finer grids, `B = 40` replicate seeds, `NPAR` workers); set `RUN_FULL <- FALSE` at the top for a quick local render. Runtime scales with the total number of fits, which the study minimises by fitting each dataset once and deriving every metric (power, FDP, recall) from that fit, and which the `bplapply`-over-datasets layout spreads evenly across cores — so it is dominated by the largest `S/n_per` fits in Parts B&C. All simulation parameters (`sd_patient`, `beta`, `sd_slope`, `n_genes`, `prop_nonnull`) are exposed as generator arguments and can be swept further to probe other regimes (e.g. weaker effects, higher between-sample noise).

